window.docContents[103].resolve({"/Type-systems/Subtyping-and-inference/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Subtyping-and-inference":{"contents":"Subtyping is the rule Subsumption of , the relation . It is the only conversion between pointers to classes. No typing rule derives  from . The rules that require  apply it instead, in declarations, assignments, arguments, results, comparisons and the branches of ?:.Subtype polymorphism and parametric polymorphism do not compose in the subset. Two instantiations of one template have no subtype relation, so a Stack<D*>* is not accepted where a Stack<B*>* is expected. Such a conversion would be unsound, because a Stack<B*> accepts a push of any B* and a Stack<D*> does not. Variance is left out of the subset.\n\nType inference in Core C++ is local. The rule of auto is T-Auto of , and it covers pointers to instantiations, since a pointer has values.A lambda has no type of its own, so it never initialises an auto, which the grammar already guarantees. Core C++ has no inference over a whole program, such as the type inference of Hindley (Trans. AMS 146, 1969, pp. 29–60, doi:10.1090/S0002-9947-1969-0253905-6) and Milner (JCSS 17, 1978, pp. 348–375, doi:10.1016/0022-0000(78)90014-4).\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Type systems","header":"7.4. Subtyping and inference","id":"/Type-systems/Subtyping-and-inference/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Subtyping-and-inference"}});